Author: Gabe Castro <gabe@techworx.dev>
Date:   Wed Sep 9 16:10:11 2026 -0700

    perf(graph): batch release-commit manifest verification into 2 cat-file calls

    derive_release_commit_decision looped over every workspace package's
    canonical manifests and called manifest_version_at twice per manifest --
    once against the parent commit, once against the release commit -- each
    shelling out a separate `git show <commit>:<path>` subprocess. For a
    monorepo with N canonical manifests that was 2N sequential subprocess
    spawns to verify one release commit's claimed version roster, on top of
    the `git diff-tree` call already made earlier in the same function.

    Since only two distinct commits are ever queried, both are now fetched
    with exactly one `git cat-file --batch` invocation each: every requested
    manifest path is written to the child's stdin up front, and each object's
    blob content (or `missing`, for a manifest that didn't exist yet at the
    parent commit) streams back on stdout in one round trip.

    CommandRunner gains a new run_with_stdin method (default: Unsupported, so
    none of the ~30 existing test-double/moon CommandRunner implementors need
    to change) that CliCommandRunner implements for real, piping input on a
    dedicated writer thread concurrently with draining the child's
    stdout/stderr so a payload larger than a pipe buffer can't deadlock.

    Because this feeds release-commit trust verification, the batch-output
    parser is deliberately defensive rather than permissive: each response is
    matched against its exact requested commit:path object string (not a
    generic pattern), a declared content length that doesn't fit the
    remaining output or isn't followed by the protocol's separator byte fails
    the whole batch closed, and any leftover/truncated output once every
    requested path is accounted for is rejected rather than ignored.

    manifest_version_at's parsing logic is extracted into parse_manifest_version,
    shared by the new batch path and (relocated into #[cfg(test)], since
    production code no longer calls the per-object form) the two existing
    tests pinning its fail-closed/fallback behavior.

    New tests: a recording CommandRunner double drives
    derive_release_commit_decision across three canonical manifests and
    asserts exactly 2 cat-file --batch invocations occur, not 6; runner.rs
    gains direct coverage of run_with_stdin (round-trip, large-payload
    deadlock avoidance, NotFound). All 7 durable-release E2E tests (real git,
    real CLI binary, including the fixed-group multi-manifest scenario and
    the tampered-decision rejection case) still pass unchanged.

    cargo test -p callisto-graph -p callisto-cli -p callisto-model: all green.
    cargo clippy -p callisto-graph -p callisto-cli -p callisto-model
    --all-targets -- -D warnings: clean.

